 "verdicts": {
  "1_forecast_p90_issue_time": "PASSES every evaluation window at 0.623x static; the only autoscaling arm that does. Win survives the near-symmetric strict-re-warm sensitivity (0.617x, 0 failures).",
  "2_reactive_react_point": "FAILS burst3. Its 16,083.740 ms is partly a readiness-model artifact: the same policy under the forecast arm's own warm-pool actuation reads 1,999.773 ms \u2014 still a failure, 8x smaller.",
  "3_reactive_plus_warm_floor_M4": "FAILS burst3 at 2,083.830 ms and 0.863x static. Under warm-pool readiness M=4 passes at 0.887x \u2014 still dearer than forecast. No floor in the family reaches forecast's cost.",
  "4_static_C8_deployable": "PASSES every evaluation window at 1.000x static by construction; the cost reference.",
  "5_perfect_future_oracle_H0": "0.292x static. As shipped it fails burst3, but ONLY through a window-boundary clamp the forecast arm does not carry; with that single asymmetry repaired it passes every evaluation window at the same 0.292x. It is the frontier leader and the perfect-information reference (not a schedule-optimal lower bound)."
 },
 "caveats": [
  "the 1.67x headline is NOT robust to the readiness-model repair \u2014 it collapses to 1.20x. The defensible claim is 'forecast is 1.2-1.7x cheaper than the best warm-floor reactive at equal compliance'.",
  "'even the perfect-future oracle fails burst3' is FALSE and must not be published: the clamped oracle's burst3 failure is a window-boundary artifact; the warm-start oracle passes every evaluation window at the same aggregate cost.",
  "'buying enough standing capacity costs more than the static fleet' holds only under the strict-re-warm actuation; under warm-pool the M=2 floor passes at 0.746x, cheaper than static. The conclusion (a floor alone never reaches forecast's cost) is unaffected.",
  "the comparison is conservative in reactive's favour: forecast is a single pre-frozen arm (rho=15 chosen ex post earlier, disclosed), the floor family is an ex-post best-of-family read on the evaluation span.",
  "R2 is decided entirely by the L=300 s calibration column; the arm is deployed at L=30 s.",
  "p99 cross-quoting: the forecast and reactive aggregate p99s recomputed here (589.301 ms) are EXACTLY the archived / DB-ingested values (provenance run_ids encoder_hybrid:fc and encoder_hybrid:re, 589.301 ms) \u2014 no fork. The manuscript's '588 ms plateau' label (tex/closedloop.tex:441,455-461) traces to a 588.37 ms measurement from a different EncoderCostModel build (0.158% below), which the manuscript itself footnotes as the '588/589 ms floor' (tex/closedloop.tex:534). Static IS forked: 646.943 ms here vs the archived rerun_verify:week_static_C8 644.440 ms (+0.389%) and vs the table's 644 (+0.457%) \u2014 inside the repo's declared 0.5% bound. All worker-seconds and cost ratios match the manuscript and the DB exactly (2,410,754.7 / 2,611,319.7 / 3,869,278.8).",
  "single trace; rho=15 and the 15-s control bin are not swept here."
